---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const points = [
  { t: 'Snapshot everything', d: 'No chart is ever drawn from a live API. Every source is fetched once, archived at a fixed vintage with a recorded checksum, and the page is built from that snapshot, so any figure can be reproduced exactly, later, by anyone.' },
  { t: 'Never invent a number', d: 'Every quantitative claim traces to a derived series you can download under its chart, with full lineage. Where a “World” line doesn’t exist in the source, we build one and record the recipe. The data is open; the working is shown.' },
  { t: 'Smoothing is cosmetic', d: 'Lines use monotone-cubic interpolation for legibility only. It never moves, adds, or hides a data point, and it cannot overshoot a real value.' },
  { t: 'The verdict is editorial', d: 'The hope / despair / confusion reading is an honest editorial judgement of the signals, deliberately not a single computed index. Where a choice is a judgement rather than a measurement, the article says so.' },
  { t: 'Re-host only what is open', d: 'We re-publish data only under CC BY or CC0. Sources that restrict redistribution are cited and linked, never copied. Attribution is generated from the source registry, never hand-typed.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Method — Hope & Despair" description="An honest note on what numbers cannot do, and the rules that turn a source into a chart.">
  <article class="page">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>Nothing here is a black box.</h1>
      <p class="lead">A method, though, is also a set of beliefs about what a number can and cannot carry; so this
        page begins there, and then sets out the rules that turn a source into a chart.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="musing">
      <h2>On the limits of counting</h2>
      <p class="open">A number is a kind of light. In a dark room it gives you the shape of a thing: its size, its
        direction, the speed it moves. It gives you none of its colour, and none of its grief.</p>
      <p>This atlas is built almost entirely from counts, so it is worth saying plainly what a count cannot hold.
        <em>“Not everything that can be counted counts, and not everything that counts can be counted,”</em> wrote
        the sociologist William Bruce Cameron in 1963. The line is so reliably hung on Einstein, who never said it,
        that its own false attribution has become a small lesson in measuring the wrong thing. A death becomes a
        rate per 100,000; a life becomes a point on a line that does not know its name. The chart buys its clarity
        at exactly that price, and the price is never printed on the chart.</p>
      <p>Counts deceive upward as readily as down. A global average is a mercy and a lie at once: it can climb
        serenely while a single country burns beneath it, can report a comfort assembled out of someone else’s
        catastrophe. <em>“The map is not the territory,”</em> Alfred Korzybski warned, and one world line is the
        smallest, palest map into which eight billion separate lives have ever been folded.</p>
      <p>And still we count, because the alternative is worse. <em>“All models are wrong, but some are useful,”</em>
        the statistician George Box allowed, and the whole of this site lives inside that second clause. Take the
        numbers away and only anecdote and mood remain, and mood is the exact product a decade of headlines is built
        to sell. A count is a lamp raised in a large dark room. It will never show you the room. It will keep you
        from walking into the walls, and now and then it catches, for a moment, the shape of a door.</p>
      <p>So we try to keep faith with both at once: to count carefully, cite everything, show the working, and to
        leave confusion standing as a reading in its own right, never a footnote. The most honest thing a
        measurement can offer is sometimes only this — that here, at the edge of the lamplight, it can no longer see.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="practice">
      <h2>How the numbers are handled</h2>
      <ol class="rules">
        {points.map((p, i) => (
          <li>
            <span class="n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div><h3>{p.t}</h3><p>{p.d}</p></div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Back to the atlas</a> <span class="sep">·</span> <a href="/sources">Every source ↗</a> <span class="sep">·</span> <a href="/license">License →</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page { max-width: 44rem; padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5.5rem) 2rem; }
  .head .eyebrow { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.74rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .head h1 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 620; font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.4rem); line-height: 1.06; letter-spacing: -0.032em; margin-top: 1.1rem; }
  .lead { max-width: var(--measure); font-family: var(--serif); font-size: var(--fs-lede); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 1.4rem; text-wrap: pretty; }

  /* the philosophical frame — why we count, and humbly — comes first */
  .musing { margin-top: clamp(2.6rem, 6vh, 3.8rem); padding-top: clamp(2rem, 4.5vh, 2.8rem); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
  .musing h2 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 600; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 1.95rem); letter-spacing: -0.022em; color: var(--ink); }
  .musing .open { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: var(--fs-lede); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink); max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 1.2rem; text-wrap: pretty; }
  .musing p { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: var(--fs-body); line-height: var(--lh-body); color: var(--ink-2); max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 1.05rem; text-wrap: pretty; }
  .musing p em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }

  /* then the practice */
  .practice { margin-top: clamp(3rem, 7vh, 4.6rem); padding-top: clamp(2rem, 4.5vh, 2.8rem); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
  .practice > h2 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 600; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 1.95rem); letter-spacing: -0.022em; color: var(--ink); }
  .rules { list-style: none; margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 4vh, 2.4rem); }
  .rules li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr; gap: 1.4rem; padding: 1.6rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .rules li:first-child { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
  .rules .n { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 1.4rem; color: var(--hope); line-height: 1; }
  .rules h3 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 580; font-size: 1.2rem; letter-spacing: -0.018em; color: var(--ink); }
  .rules p { max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: var(--fs-body); line-height: var(--lh-body); color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 450; text-wrap: pretty; }

  .back { margin-top: clamp(2.8rem, 6vh, 3.8rem); font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .back a { color: var(--ink-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
  .back a:hover { color: var(--hope); border-color: var(--hope); }
  .back .sep { margin: 0 0.4em; color: var(--line-2); }
</style>
